<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=208121.

Folder publish profile for the faculty deployment.

TWO SETTINGS HERE ARE PRODUCTION-CRITICAL, and both were wrong:

  PublishUrl targeted bin\Release\net6.0\publish\ while the project has been
  net10.0 for some time, so the profile pointed at a directory the build never
  writes. Publishing through it produced, or reused, output that did not
  correspond to this project.

  DeleteExistingFiles was false, which is exactly the setting that causes the
  stale-asset failure documented in CLAUDE.md: Blazor emits content-hashed
  files under _framework/, and publishing over an existing directory leaves
  every previous build's hashed .wasm/.pdb behind. The browser then loads a
  blazor.boot.json listing one set of hashes while the folder also holds
  several older sets - and any cached index.html requests a hash that may have
  been pruned, which is the "Loading..." hang that 404s on a file that looks
  like it should exist. The publish directory must be built clean every time.

LaunchSiteAfterPublish is off: publishing to a folder for hand-off to a server
should not start anything locally.

The database is deliberately NOT part of publish output (*.db is git-ignored
and no project item includes it). Copy Server/FinalProjectDB.db to the server
by hand, or point ConnectionStrings:DefaultConnection at an absolute path
outside the deployed directory so a redeploy cannot touch it. See
docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DeleteExistingFiles>true</DeleteExistingFiles>
    <ExcludeApp_Data>false</ExcludeApp_Data>
    <LaunchSiteAfterPublish>false</LaunchSiteAfterPublish>
    <LastUsedBuildConfiguration>Release</LastUsedBuildConfiguration>
    <LastUsedPlatform>Any CPU</LastUsedPlatform>
    <PublishProvider>FileSystem</PublishProvider>
    <PublishUrl>bin\Release\net10.0\publish\</PublishUrl>
    <WebPublishMethod>FileSystem</WebPublishMethod>
    <_TargetId>Folder</_TargetId>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
